Tests are written in the Ruby programming language using the
Test::Unit framework. The Parslet library is used to parse responses
from the ToastStunt server under test.

To run the tests:

1) Ensure you have Ruby 1.9.3 or later installed.

2) Ensure you have Bundler installed.

3) Install dependencies.
    bundle install

4) Create the following symlinks in the executables/ directory:
    ln -s `which echo` echo
    ln -s `which sleep` sleep
    ln -s `which true` true

5) Build the server and copy the executable into the test directory.

6) Verify the settings in test.yml are accurate for your configuration.

7) In one console/terminal/window, run the ToastStunt server in the test directory:
    ./moo Test.db /tmp/toast-test-out.db 9898

8) In another console/terminal/window, run the tests:
    make

9) Clean up the moo executable and the files created in /tmp:
    make clean

Native map-key ordering regressions can run in an isolated Test.db process:

    python3 test/run_map_order_regressions.py build/moo

From the source root, test loading a checkpoint written by the old binary:

    python3 test/run_map_order_regressions.py build/moo --reload-from /path/to/old/moo

The reload test first proves lookup failures in the old runtime, then checks
all stored numeric, boolean, waif and anonymous keys after two fixed-runtime
reloads. It uses a temporary database and an ephemeral localhost port.
Run the direct comparator invariant check with the command at the top of
test/test_numeric_key_compare.cc after configuring the build with CMake.

Ordered string-line validation and scalar-semantics bulk FileIO regressions:

    python3 test/run_map_order_regressions.py build/moo --test test_string_lines.rb

This uses the same isolated server/Ruby framework. Write/flush fault controls
require POSIX /dev/full. See docs/Features/string_lines.md for the API contract.

Maintained WAIF owner allocation counts and deferred-finalizer controls:

    python3 test/run_map_order_regressions.py build/moo --test test_waif_owner_count.rb
    python3 test/run_waif_owner_count_reload.py build/moo --reload-from /path/to/old/moo

The reload test verifies distinct allocations and saved native continuations
across two candidate-runtime reloads. See docs/Features/waif_owner_count.md.

Recycled object slots still referenced by allocated WAIF owners or classes:

    python3 test/run_map_order_regressions.py build/moo --test test_recycled_waif_slots.rb
    python3 test/run_recycled_waif_regressions.py build/moo --prepare-with /path/to/old/moo

The tests exercise real finalization/resurrection, initializer suspension,
renumbering and high-water trimming. The reload test retains an invalid owner
across two candidate loads before actual reference release permits reuse.
See docs/Features/recycled_waif_slots.md for destination and error-order rules.

Temporary anonymous checkpoint IDs must not revive destroyed WAIF classes:

    python3 -B test/run_waif_checkpoint_alias.py build/moo
    python3 -B test/run_waif_checkpoint_alias.py build/moo --prepare-with /path/to/old/moo --recover-old

The default checks candidate writing/loading in both anonymous-first and
no-anonymous cases. The recovery mode proves old-writer counts before any
getter, then candidate resaving and a second load while preserving the outer
owner. An old writer without --recover-old intentionally reproduces the bug.
Use the existing WSL bash -lc login environment for its rbenv Ruby installation.
